 ### End-point Corrector
-A technique to address field variation divergence near skeleton end-points in Scale-Invariant Integral Surfaces (SCALIS) fields [S2]. It forces an additional sampling position near end-points to increase correlation between the minima of individual primitives and the global field [S2].
-*   **What it is:** An additive correction segment.
-*   **How it works:** Adds a segment primitive with constant radius at the end vertices of the skeleton [S2].
-*   **Parameters:** The length of the end-point corrector segment is proportional to the prescribed radius at the end vertices of the skeleton and a constant depending on the kernel used [S2].
-*   **When to choose:** Use when rendering skeletal structures where radius shrinkage at end-points causes visual artifacts or incorrect distance values [S2]. If absent, a heuristic presented in section 6.2 of the source can compensate [S2].
+A technique used in the context of Scale-Invariant Integral Surfaces (SCALIS) to address field variation divergence near skeleton end-points. It forces an additional sampling position near end-points to increase correlation between the minima of individual primitives and the global field. It is chosen when rendering skeletal structures where radius shrinkage at endpoints is visible. The implementation involves adding a segment primitive with constant radius at the end vertices of the skeleton. The length of this corrector segment is proportional to the prescribed radius at the end vertices and a constant depending on the kernel used [S2]. If absent, a heuristic can compensate [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query point. The calculation involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No auxiliary data structures.
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently creates complex tapered shapes without requiring mesh generation or high-resolution voxel grids.
-*   **Limitations:** The specific formula provided is tailored to a particular primitive type (tapered cylinder/cone hybrid). Generalizing to arbitrary base primitives requires deriving the corresponding transformation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Ideal for organic modeling, skeletal structures, and procedural geometry where elongation is a primary design parameter.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query, assuming the base SDF is $O(1)$. It adds a constant number of arithmetic operations.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional memory is required.
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; allows reuse of existing base SDFs to create varied shapes; computationally cheap.
+- **Limitations:** The resulting distance field may not be perfectly smooth if the base SDF or the transformation introduces discontinuities in derivatives. The elongation is anisotropic and may distort the metric in ways that affect ray marching step sizes if not accounted for.
+- **When to use:** When creating stretched primitives (capsules, stretched spheres) for ray marching or volume rendering.
+- **When not to use:** When high-order continuity is required across the elongated regions, or when the elongation vector is dynamic and causes frequent re-evaluation of complex base SDFs.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of elongation in SDFs is rooted in the manipulation of domain space to create non-uniform scaling effects. The specific implementation detailed in [S1, unofficial] appears in the context of Torch-Sphere-Tracer, a library for differentiable sphere tracing. The End-point Corrector variant originates from research on Scale-Invariant Integral Surfaces (SCALIS) [S2], specifically addressing the mathematical limitations of integral surface representations near the boundaries of skeletal structures.
+The elongation operator is a standard technique in procedural geometry and implicit surface rendering. The specific formulation described in [S1, unofficial] (unofficial) is part of a collection of SDF primitives and transformations implemented in a Python-based sphere tracer. The end-point corrector variant is described in the context of ray tracing scale-invariant integral surfaces [S2].
 
